cher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The input contains two kinds of object: solid 5×5 squares, and small 3×3 glyphs drawn in single colours.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- **What survives into the output.** The output keeps only the solid squares, with the one-cell background margin around their arrangement. In pair 1 that is the column of squares 1, 6 and 4. In pair 2 it is the row of squares 1, 2 and 3. In pair 3 it is the 2×2 block of squares 8, 4, 3 and 9.\n- **What changes inside a square.** Every square now has a pattern in its inner 3×3. That pattern is the glyph of the same colour, inverted:\n  - In pair 1 the 1-glyph is `111 / .1. / 111`. The inside of square 1 becomes background where the glyph has paint, and keeps colour 1 where the glyph is empty.\n  - The 6-glyph `6.6 / 66. / 6..` becomes `.6. / ..6 / .66` inside square 6.\n  - The glyph is never rotated or mirrored.\n- **Glyphs that are not used.** Glyphs whose colour has no square (3, 5, 9 in pair 1, for example) are simply dropped.\n\n**Rule.**\n1. Find the solid 5×5 squares.\n2. For each square of colour c, take the bounding box of all other c-coloured cells. That box is the glyph. I do not use connected components, because some glyphs are disconnected, for example the 3-glyph in pair 1.\n3. Paint background into the square's inner 3×3 wherever the glyph has a cell.\n4. Crop the bounding box of the squares, extended by one cell on every side.\n\n**Test.** There are six squares (4, 8, 2, 1, 6, 9), arranged 3 rows × 2 columns at x = 11..21. Each has a matching glyph on the left side, so the output is 19×13.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    squares = []\n    for o in objects(g, bg=bg):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if y1 - y0 == 4 and x1 - x0 == 4 and o[\"size\"] == 25:\n            squares.append(o)\n    inSq = np.zeros(g.shape, bool)\n    for o in squares:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        inSq[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1] = True\n    out = g.copy()\n    for o in squares:\n        c = o[\"colour\"]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        cells = list(zip(*np.nonzero((g == c) & ~inSq)))\n        if not cells:\n            continue\n        gy0, gx0, gy1, gx1 = bbox(cells)\n        for (y, x) in cells:\n            dy, dx = y - gy0, x - gx0\n            if dy < 3 and dx < 3:\n                out[y0 + 1 + dy, x0 + 1 + dx] = bg\n        emit(out, f\"Stamped the colour-{c} glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-{c} square.\")\n    ys = [o[\"box\"][0] for o in squares] + [o[\"box\"][2] for o in squares]\n    xs = [o[\"box\"][1] for o in squares] + [o[\"box\"][3] for o in squares]\n    Y0, Y1 = max(min(ys) - 1, 0), min(max(ys) + 1, H - 1)\n    X0, X1 = max(min(xs) - 1, 0), min(max(xs) + 1, W - 1)\n    res = out[Y0:Y1+1, X0:X1+1]\n    emit(res, \"Cropped to the block of big squares plus a one-cell border; leftover glyphs are discarded.\")\n    return res\n","candidate_tactics":{"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The input contains two kinds of object: solid 5×5 squares, and small 3×3 glyphs drawn in single colours."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That pattern is the glyph of the same colour, inverted:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In pair 1 the 1-glyph is `111 / .1. / 111`."}],"template":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-1 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-1 square."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-6 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-6 square."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-4 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-4 square."}],"topology":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-1 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-1 square."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-6 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-6 square."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamped the colour-4 glyph as background holes into the inner 3x3 of the colour-4 square."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each square of colour c, take the bounding box of all other c-coloured cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop the bounding box of the squares, extended by one cell on every side."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Cropped to the block of big squares plus a one-cell border; leftover glyphs are discarded."}],"instructions":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The input contains two kinds of object: solid 5×5 squares, and small 3×3 glyphs drawn in single colours."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That pattern is the glyph of the same colour, inverted:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- In pair 1 the 1-glyph is `111 / .1. / 111`."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"e64a84feba1cf7be8fc507055f94e51e90ed479cb44060860ea6e655550a8579","task_sha256":"8eed301948b3a8d3db61c29c9bffe20c90cbc9c798b75fb9d1be70b97d95de1f","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
